          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p18\",\"quote\":\"216 Kleopatra , with a mean diameter of 122 km, is the third largest M-type asteroid known after 16 Psyche and 22 Kalliope. Radar delay-Doppler imaging, high-resolution telescopic images, and several stellar occultations show it to be a contact binary asteroid with a shape commonly referred to as a \\\"dog-bone\\\" or \\\"dumbbell.\\\"\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p7\",\"quote\":\"In a few cases, astronomers have managed to develop three-dimensional shape models using a variety of techniques (cf. 16 Psyche or 216 Kleopatra for examples)\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p8\",\"quote\":\"Size estimates based on shape models (usually derived from adaptive optics, occultations, and radar imaging) are the most reliable.\"}]}"
